Motivation
----------
A structured-output grammar often knows the next k tokens with certainty:
wherever its token bitmask has cardinality 1, any sampler must emit that
one token, yet each such token still costs a full engine step. Those
forced runs (JSON structural keys, SQL keywords/punctuation, closing
brackets) are exactly the compute shape speculative decoding already
handles: verify k proposed tokens under per-position bitmasks in one
forward pass. A grammar-proposed forced run is a draft with GUARANTEED
acceptance — at each draft position the bitmask admits exactly one token,
so verification cannot reject it under any sampling configuration
(temperature, top-p, seeded multinomial). No drafter model is required.

Mechanism
---------
1. StructuredOutputGrammar.jump_tokens() -> list[int], default [] (non-
   abstract: existing backends are unaffected). Contract: the returned ids
   are, position by position, the ONLY token the grammar's own
   fill_bitmask would allow after the previous ones; the call is
   state-neutral (the manager advances/rolls back the grammar itself
   around per-position fills, exactly as it does for drafter proposals).
2. Scheduler.update_from_output: after grammar.accept_tokens, install
   jump_tokens() as request.spec_token_ids. When a drafter proposal
   exists for the request, the existing validate_tokens assignment below
   overwrites it — proposer behavior is unchanged.
3. No new config: backends that do not override jump_tokens keep [] and
   nothing changes; scheduling/verification of the injected ids reuses the
   existing spec-token plumbing (scheduled_spec_decode_tokens,
   per-position bitmask rows, rejection-sampler acceptance).

Soundness: injected ids are verified under the grammar's own per-position
bitmasks like any draft; a buggy backend span is rejected at verification,
never emitted. Distribution-exactness at forced positions is trivial (the
constrained distribution is a point mass).
